1. Legacy membership persistence: when building a job's member set for a poll, merge the retained (pid, starttime) history of EVERY previously known member of that job, whichever member happens to initialise the job entry first (the setdefault() at bin/memguard:212 only copies history when the first member is known). Members that are temporarily ineligible (e.g. comm became node) must stay in the job's known-member history with their starttime, so they are re-tracked and killed if they become eligible again with the same starttime; they must never be signalled while ineligible. The survivor inventory uses this same history. Add a deterministic mocked regression spanning >=3 polls that reproduces the review's exact scenario (legacy job knows 10,12,13; 11 protected node; wrapper 10 exits; 11 becomes eligible python; 13 temporarily node; 13 returns to python with the same starttime and calls setsid) and asserts the kill reports 13 (killed or survivor), never survivors=[] with 13 alive.
2. Watchdog mutual exclusion: a memguard process and its bin/job wrapper must NEVER be eligible, in any instance. Treat as protected any process whose GEMMA_JOB_ID / job name starts with "memguard" (exact prefix compare), OR whose cmdline runs bin/memguard (argv[0] or argv[1] basename == "memguard"), OR that is the bin/job wrapper (parent) of such a process. This must hold for both the new job-ID jobs and legacy jobs without GEMMA_JOB_ID (the currently deployed old guard runs as legacy job "memguard"). Mocked tests: a snapshot with an old-style legacy memguard job plus the new memguard-light job: the new guard targets neither (no oom_score_adj write, no signal, not counted as a victim). We will NOT run old and new guards concurrently (the deploy is: stop old, then start new, with no heavy jobs running), so no test against the old implementation is needed; state this in a comment at the exclusion code.
